[ROCm] Add a K-loop schedule for dense fp16 GEMM on gfx90a

LLVM clusters global loads and moves memory operations out of MFMA runs, and no
source form of the K loop keeps them apart. T.Kernel(..., gemm_schedule="pgr2")
asks for a schedule in the layout of Tensile's PGR2 instead: memory operations
between MFMAs, barriers that do not stop math, accumulators in AGPRs, padded LDS
rows. A new pass, ScheduleGemm, recognizes the plain loop

    copy(A tile), copy(B tile), gemm(A_s, B_s, C_l, transpose_B=True)

and emits the K loop as one asm block, for 256x128x64 and 128x256x64 tiles.
Anything it does not recognize compiles as usual, with a warning giving the reason.
The schedule itself was designed by hand; the pass generates it for the shape.

Measured on MI250X, ROCm 6.2.4 and 7.2.3, randn, against hipBLASLt through torch
in the same process: 1.01 to 1.10 at 9 shapes on 2 jobs, against 0.51 to 0.71 for
the same kernel under LLVM's schedule. Output is bitwise equal to the hand kernel
this schedule came from.

